    "q-169979": {
      "dom_id": "step-q169979",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the solution $(u,t)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-u+2t=-2 \\\\ 3u-2t=10\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $t$ in the first equation is $2,$ and the coefficient of $t$ in the second equation is $-2.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $t,$ and solve the resulting equation for $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-u+\\cancel{2t} & =-2 \\\\ 3u-\\cancel{2t} & =10 \\\\ (-u+3u) & =(-2+10) \\\\ 2u & =8 \\\\ u & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u=4.$ However, we still need to find $t.$ To do this, we substitute $u=4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-u+2t & =-2 \\\\ -(4)+2t & =-2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=4,\\,t=1,$ which can be expressed as $(4,1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
