    "q-112395": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112395",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4p-5q=1 \\\\ 4p+2q=2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $-4,$ and the coefficient of $p$ in the second equation is $4.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $p,$ and solve the resulting equation for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{4p}-5q & =1 \\\\ \\cancel{4p}+2q & =2 \\\\ (-5q+2q) & =(1+2) \\\\ -3q & =3 \\\\ q & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $q=-1.$ However, we still need to find $p.$ To do this, we substitute $q=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p+2q & =2 \\\\ 4p+2(-1) & =2 \\\\ 4p-2 & =2 \\\\ 4p & =4 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=1,\\,q=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(1,-1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
