    "q-214761": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214761",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Using the elimination method, eliminate the variable $a$ and find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-5 \\\\ -2a+3b=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $2,$ and the coefficient of $a$ in the second equation is $-2.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $a,$ and solve the resulting equation for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2a}+3b & =-5 \\\\ -\\cancel{2a}+3b & =11 \\\\ (3b+3b) & =(-5+11) \\\\ 6b & =6 \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=1.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3b & =-5 \\\\ 2a+3(1) & =-5 \\\\ 2a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=1,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,-4,1\\,).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
