    "q-170022": {
      "dom_id": "step-q170022",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z=-2 \\\\ 3u-5z=-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $u$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the second equation by $-1$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}-3u+5z=17.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}⋯\\,\\,?\\,\\,⋯\\,\\,=15.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(u,z)$ of the system.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-1$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z=-2 \\\\ (\\,3u-5z=-17\\,)\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z=-2 \\\\ -3u+5z=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $u,$ and solve the resulting equation for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3u}+z & =-2 \\\\ -\\cancel{3u}+5z & =17 \\\\ (z+5z) & =(-2+17) \\\\ 6z & =15 \\\\ z & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $z=\\frac{5}{2}.$ However, we still need to find $u.$ To do this, we substitute $z=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u+z & =-2 \\\\ 3u+\\frac{5}{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3u & =-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,z=\\frac{5}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
